        ("After the index correlation structure breaks, how long does a positive Micro250 × Gold correlation sustain?", f"There is no consistent positive correlation to sustain: Micro250 × Gold was positive at the break in {int(gbe[gbe['pair'] == 'Micro250|GoldMCX']['positive_at_break'].sum())} of its {int(gbe[gbe['pair'] == 'Micro250|GoldMCX']['episode'].nunique())} episodes (June 2023, where it then stayed positive {int(gbe[gbe['pair'] == 'Micro250|GoldMCX']['longest_positive_run'].max())} weeks) and negative at the break in the rest; the median episode has it positive {gbe[gbe['pair'] == 'Micro250|GoldMCX'][f'weeks_positive_of_first_{C.GOLD_AFTER_BREAK}'].median():.0f} of the first {C.GOLD_AFTER_BREAK} weeks, scattered. The other gold pairs average near zero after a break too.", "B2 (the after-the-break chart)"),
        ("Does correlation depend on what the signal says — both BUY, disagree, both SELL?", f"Yes, a little: index pairs average {bs3.loc[idx_pairs, 'both BUY'].mean():.2f} in both-BUY weeks, {bs3.loc[idx_pairs, 'disagree'].mean():.2f} when the signals disagree, {bs3.loc[idx_pairs, 'both SELL'].mean():.2f} in both-SELL weeks. Gold's stays near zero in all three.", "B3"),
        ("Does correlation rise before a signal flips (a warning) or after (a consequence)?", f"After. Around every asset's SELL flip the group correlation is flat before and climbs ~0.1 in the two months after (N100: {af('N100', 'flip to SELL', -4):.2f} → {af('N100', 'flip to SELL', 0):.2f} → {af('N100', 'flip to SELL', 8):.2f}; on the median {af('N100', 'flip to SELL', -4, 'median_corr'):.2f} → {af('N100', 'flip to SELL', 0, 'median_corr'):.2f} → {af('N100', 'flip to SELL', 8, 'median_corr'):.2f}, and without the {EXC} flips {af('N100', 'flip to SELL', -4, f'mean_corr_ex{EXC}'):.2f} → {af('N100', 'flip to SELL', 0, f'mean_corr_ex{EXC}'):.2f} → {af('N100', 'flip to SELL', 8, f'mean_corr_ex{EXC}'):.2f} — not a COVID artefact). It is a consequence of the sell-off that fired the signal. Pair by pair, the climb is in the N100 pairs; the pairs among the smaller indices were already coupled — the rise is Nifty re-joining the block (B4b).", "B4"),
        ("Is the market more coupled when more signals are in SELL?", f"Yes, monotonically: {bb.loc['none in SELL', 'mean']:.2f} with none in SELL → {bb.loc['all in SELL', 'mean']:.2f} with all four. Pair by pair: every index pair rises with breadth, the N100 pairs most (N100×SC100 {bpd.loc['none in SELL', 'N100|SC100']:.2f} → {bpd.loc['all in SELL', 'N100|SC100']:.2f}); gold pairs stay near zero.", "B5 (table) and B6 (timeline)"),
        ("Is a SELL state a high-correlation state or a high-volatility state?", f"A high-volatility state. Swings are {vs.loc[eq, 'vol_ratio_SELL_over_BUY'].mean():.1f}× bigger in an index's own SELL weeks while correlation rises only {bs3.loc[idx_pairs, 'both SELL'].mean() - bs3.loc[idx_pairs, 'both BUY'].mean():.2f}; {eqp['share_of_cov_change_from_vol'].mean() * 100:.0f}% of the extra covariance (risk) in SELL is volatility. The medians say the same as the means (N100's typical weekly move {sdist[(sdist['asset'] == 'N100') & (sdist['state'] == 'BUY') & (sdist['measure'] == 'abs_weekly_return')]['median'].iloc[0]:.1%} in BUY vs {sdist[(sdist['asset'] == 'N100') & (sdist['state'] == 'SELL') & (sdist['measure'] == 'abs_weekly_return')]['median'].iloc[0]:.1%} in SELL), so it is not one outlier.", "B7, and B3's right panel"),
        ("Is the VIX a leading or a lagging indicator of the supertrend's SELL flip?", (f"The same event, with the VIX a few weeks ahead because the supertrend is slow by construction: the VIX is up {vfe[vfe['vix_data']]['vix_change_4w_before'].mean():+.0%} on average over the 4 weeks before a SELL flip, the flip week is the single most common VIX-surge week, and for the typical flip the VIX has peaked by then (the median falls afterwards). But a surge is a poor warning on its own: {vfs.loc[eq, 'share_surges_already_in_SELL'].mean():.0%} of surges find the index already in SELL and only {vfs.loc[eq, 'share_surges_followed_by_flip'].mean():.0%} are followed by its flip within {C.VIX_FLIP_WINDOW} weeks. The flip stays necessary — it dates the instance the index is in SELL." if has_vfs else "No VIX file."), "B7 (the VIX-vs-flip chart)"),
        ("Does correlation rise with volatility — pair by pair, whichever vol we use?", f"Yes for every index pair under all three regime definitions (N100×SC100: {regw[regimes[0]].loc['N100|SC100', 'Q1']:.2f} in the calmest fifth of weeks → {regw[regimes[0]].loc['N100|SC100', 'Q5']:.2f} in the wildest). Gold's correlation does not rise.", "B8"),
        ("Which moves first — volatility or correlation?", f"Neither — and the redone test with no overlapping weeks (2026-10-19) removes even the VIX's apparent head start, which was the overlap: on 4- and 13-week blocks that share no week, the VIX's change predicts the next block's correlation change in {sum(1 for p in [q for q in pst.index if 'Gold' not in q] for L in C.LL_BLOCKS for g in [ll[(ll['pair'] == p) & (ll['direction'] == 'VIX → correlation') & (ll['kind'] == 'change') & (ll['block_weeks'] == L)]] if len(g) and abs(g['nw_t'].iloc[0]) >= 2 and np.sign(g['pearson'].iloc[0]) == np.sign(g['pearson_nonoverlap'].iloc[0]))} of {sum(1 for p in [q for q in pst.index if 'Gold' not in q] for L in C.LL_BLOCKS if len(ll[(ll['pair'] == p) & (ll['direction'] == 'VIX → correlation') & (ll['kind'] == 'change') & (ll['block_weeks'] == L)]))} index-pair tests, and the pair's own volatility never leads its correlation upward. What is left is negative both ways: after a block in which both spiked, both ease. In the correlation jumps all three — correlation, realised vol, the VIX — were at their usual level four weeks before and high together in the jump week; at the pairs' own jumps the vol was above +1 beforehand in only {own.loc[[p for p in own.index if 'Gold' not in p], 'share_z_vol_above1_before'].mean():.0%}. The gold pairs' correlation does not move with volatility at all.", "B9"),
        ("When correlation is high, what is the market doing — and what does it do next?", f"Falling, then recovering. The most-correlated fifth of weeks coincided with N100 {_pct(qn['N100'].loc['Q5', 'same_quarter_mean'])} over that quarter and was followed by {_pct(qn['N100'].loc['Q5', f'next_{F4}w_mean'])} over the next {F4} weeks (positive {qn['N100'].loc['Q5', f'next_{F4}w_hit']:.0%} of the time) and {_pct(qn['N100'].loc['Q5', 'next_quarter_mean'])} over the next quarter (positive {qn['N100'].loc['Q5', 'next_quarter_hit']:.0%}); the least-correlated fifth: {_pct(qn['N100'].loc['Q1', 'same_quarter_mean'])} then {_pct(qn['N100'].loc['Q1', f'next_{F4}w_mean'])} / {_pct(qn['N100'].loc['Q1', 'next_quarter_mean'])}. The gradient is flatter at {F4} weeks than at 13 — the recovery takes a quarter to show. Pair by pair the same shape holds in each index pair with a full history; gold's own next weeks are flat whichever index it happened to move with.", "B10, B11, B12"),
        ("Does the ratio of one leg to the other — who wins — respond to the correlation, the signal, or its own recent move?", f"No. The ratio's change over the next {C.RATIO_FWD} weeks is within about ±1% in every correlation quintile and every joint signal state of every full-history pair, with hit rates around a coin flip; the ratio's last {C.RATIO_BACK} weeks say nothing about its next {C.RATIO_FWD} (rank correlation {rs[rs['cut'] == 'all weeks'].set_index('pair').loc[[p for p in pst.index if 'Micro250' not in p], 'spearman_fwd_vs_past'].min():+.2f} to {rs[rs['cut'] == 'all weeks'].set_index('pair').loc[[p for p in pst.index if 'Micro250' not in p], 'spearman_fwd_vs_past'].max():+.2f}). Correlation and the signal describe how the two legs move together, not which one wins.", "B10b"),
        ("Which index breaks the correlation first, and which way?", f"The smaller index, by running ahead ({date_first} most often by date); Nifty is the one left behind (by size of collapse, {drop_first}). {sm.loc['MC100', 'share_up_breaks'] * 100:.0f}% of Midcap's and {sm.loc['SC100', 'share_up_breaks'] * 100:.0f}% of Smallcap's breaks are upward, {100 - sm.loc['N100', 'share_up_breaks'] * 100:.0f}% of Nifty's downward. An index that decoupled by outperforming returned {_pct(up.loc[13, 'breaker'])} over the next quarter — the run was ending.", "B13, B14"),
        ("In a price sell-off, who falls first and whose signal fires first?", f"{first_cross} crosses −10% first ({sm4.loc[first_cross, 'first_share_cross_10pct'] * 100:.0f}% of sell-offs) and falls most; {first_sig}'s supertrend fires first ({sm4.loc[first_sig, 'first_share_signal_sell'] * 100:.0f}%).", "B15"),
        ("Do deviations between two assets pull back? Is there a pair-trading strategy?", f"No. {10 - len(coint_ok)} of 10 price ratios have no long-run level; a 1–2 sd deviation continued ({_pct(pz.loc[B[1], 'mean_ratio_change'])} further over 13 weeks after a lag, t {_t(pz.loc[B[1], 'hac_t_ratio_change'])}); the classic pair trade lost in 10 of 10 pairs ({int(ptall['trades'])} trades, {_pct(ptall['mean_net'])} each).", "B16, B17, B18"),
